content.text:
Fixed. Here's what was wrong and what changed.

## Root cause

MCP servers are loaded the same way in cloud and self-hosted mode — `McpService._loadConfig()` reads `agent/mcps/mcp.json` and expands `${VAR}` placeholders via `replaceEnvVars()`. But `replaceEnvVars()` only looked at `process.env`.

In **cloud mode**, per-project env vars (e.g. `METABASE_URL`, `METABASE_API_KEY`) are stored in the database on `project.envVars` and set through the project settings UI — they are never in the container's `process.env`. So the placeholders never resolved:

- HTTP servers with a `${VAR}` URL failed Zod validation, and the `catch` block dropped the **entire** config to `{}` → nothing detected.
- stdio servers (metabase) got literal unresolved values and failed discovery → "not detected".

SQL execution already merges these DB env vars; MCP loading was the one path that didn't.

## Fix

Two changes in `apps/backend`:

- `src/utils/utils.ts` — `replaceEnvVars` now takes an optional `extraEnv` that overrides `process.env`:

```119:125:apps/backend/src/utils/utils.ts
export const replaceEnvVars = (fileContent: string, extraEnv: Record<string, string | undefined> = {}) => {
	const env = { ...process.env, ...extraEnv };
	const replaced = fileContent.replace(/\$\{(\w+)\}/g, (match, varName) => {
		return env[varName] || match;
	});
	return replaced;
};
```

- `src/services/mcp.ts` — `_loadConfig()` now merges the project's DB env vars before resolving placeholders (and imports `getEnvVars`):

```405:410:apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts
		try {
			const fileContent = await readFile(this._mcpJsonFilePath, 'utf8');
			const projectEnvVars = this._projectId ? await getEnvVars(this._projectId) : {};
			const resolved = replaceEnvVars(fileContent, projectEnvVars);
			const parsed = mcpJsonSchema.parse(JSON.parse(resolved));
			this._mcpServers = parsed.mcpServers;
```

This also benefits self-hosted users who configure env vars via the settings UI instead of the container environment.

Backend typecheck (`tsc --noEmit -p apps/backend`) passes and there are no linter errors on the touched files.

One thing worth flagging: this fix covers configs that reference `${VAR}` placeholders. If your cloud MCP servers still don't appear after deploying, the other thing to double-check is that `agent/mcps/mcp.json` is actually included in the deployed tarball (`nao deploy`) — a missing file silently yields an empty server list too. Want me to look into the deploy packaging as well?
